IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `sscba-types`

-- Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992 (UK) — GOALS 1-3:
-- CLASS 1 CONTRIBUTIONS, STATUTORY SICK PAY, STATUTORY MATERNITY PAY.
-- Source: ../registers/source-bundle/ukpga-1992-4.txt.

GIVEN a IS A NUMBER, b IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lesser of` a b MEANS IF a AT MOST b THEN a ELSE b

GIVEN a IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`not below nothing` a MEANS IF a GREATER THAN 0 THEN a ELSE 0

§ `Goal 1 — Class 1 contributions`

-- s 8(1) "the aggregate of— (a) the main primary percentage of so much of
-- the earner's earnings paid in the tax week ... as— (i) exceeds the current
-- primary threshold ...; but (ii) does not exceed the current upper earnings
-- limit ...; and (b) the additional primary percentage of so much of those
-- earnings as exceeds the current upper earnings limit". (2) main 8%,
-- additional 2%.
@ref s 8(1)-(2)
@export The primary Class 1 contribution on a week's earnings under s 8 of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992 (UK): 8% of earnings above the primary threshold and up to the upper earnings limit, plus 2% of earnings above the upper earnings limit. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN w IS A `Week of earnings`  @desc The earnings and the weekly primary threshold and upper earnings limit
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the primary Class 1 contribution on` w MEANS
        0.08 TIMES `not below nothing` (`the lesser of` (w's `earnings`) (w's `upper earnings limit`) MINUS w's `primary threshold`)
    PLUS 0.02 TIMES `not below nothing` (w's `earnings` MINUS w's `upper earnings limit`)

-- s 9(1) "the relevant percentage of so much of the earnings paid in the tax
-- week ... as exceeds the current secondary threshold". (2) 15%.
@ref s 9(1)-(2)
@export The secondary Class 1 contribution on a week's earnings under s 9 of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992 (UK): the relevant percentage (15%, or the age-related or apprentice percentage) of earnings above the secondary threshold. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN w IS A `Week of earnings`  @desc The earnings, the secondary threshold and the relevant percentage
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the secondary Class 1 contribution on` w MEANS
    w's `secondary percentage` DIVIDED BY 100 TIMES `not below nothing` (w's `earnings` MINUS w's `secondary threshold`)

§ `Goal 2 — statutory sick pay`

-- s 157(1) "the lower of— (a) £123.25, and (b) 80% of the employee's normal
-- weekly earnings."
@ref s 157(1)
@export The weekly rate of statutory sick pay under s 157(1) of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992 (UK): the lower of £123.25 and 80% of normal weekly earnings. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN s IS A `Sick pay facts`  @desc Normal weekly earnings
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the weekly rate of statutory sick pay for` s MEANS
    `the lesser of` 123.25 (s's `normal weekly earnings` TIMES 0.8)

-- s 157(3) "The amount ... in respect of any day shall be the weekly rate ...
-- divided by the number of days which are, in the week ... in which that day
-- falls, qualifying days".
@ref s 157(3)
@export The statutory sick pay for one qualifying day under s 157(3) of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992 (UK): the weekly rate divided by the number of qualifying days in that week. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN s IS A `Sick pay facts`  @desc Normal weekly earnings and the qualifying days in the week
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the statutory sick pay for a day for` s MEANS
    `the weekly rate of statutory sick pay for` s DIVIDED BY s's `qualifying days in the week`

-- s 155(3)-(4): the maximum entitlement is reached when SSP "first reaches or
-- passes the entitlement limit", "28 times the weekly rate".
@ref s 155(2)-(4)
GIVEN s IS A `Sick pay facts`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the entitlement limit for` s MEANS
    28 TIMES `the weekly rate of statutory sick pay for` s

-- s 152(3) "Any two periods of incapacity for work which are separated by a
-- period of not more than 8 weeks shall be treated as a single period".
@ref s 152(3)
@export Whether two periods of incapacity for work are treated as a single period under s 152(3) of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992 (UK): the days between them number not more than 8 weeks (56 days). DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN s IS A `Sick pay facts`  @desc The last day of the earlier period and the first day of the later one
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the periods of incapacity link for` s IF
    CONSIDER s's `earlier period ended on`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST e  THEN Day (s's `later period began on`) MINUS Day e MINUS 1 AT MOST 56

§ `Goal 3 — statutory maternity pay`

-- s 171(1A): a week is seven days beginning with Sunday. The expected week
-- of confinement begins on the Sunday on or before the expected date.
@ref s 171(1A)
GIVEN m IS A `Maternity facts`
GIVETH A DATE
`the expected week of confinement begins for` m MEANS
    Date (Day d MINUS `Weekday of` d)
    WHERE
        d MEANS m's `expected date of confinement`

GIVEN n IS A NUMBER, m IS A `Maternity facts`
GIVETH A DATE
`the start of week` n `before the expected week for` m MEANS
    Date (Day (`the expected week of confinement begins for` m) MINUS 7 TIMES n)

-- s 164(2) "(a) ... employed earner's employment ... for a continuous period
-- of at least 26 weeks ending with the week immediately preceding the 14th
-- week before the expected week of confinement ...; (b) ... normal weekly
-- earnings for the period of 8 weeks ending with [that week] are not less
-- than the lower earnings limit ...; (c) ... has reached, or been confined
-- before reaching, the commencement of the 11th week before the expected week
-- of confinement."
@ref s 164(2)
@export Whether a woman is entitled to statutory maternity pay under s 164(2) of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992 (UK) on a date: 26 weeks' continuous employment ending with the 15th week before the expected week of confinement (and still employed then), normal weekly earnings not less than the lower earnings limit, and having reached (or been confined before) the start of the 11th week before. DRAFT, not reviewed by a UK lawyer.
GIVEN m IS A `Maternity facts`  @desc The expected date of confinement, employment start, earnings and the lower earnings limit, and the date in question
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is entitled to statutory maternity pay` m IF
        m's `employment began on` AT MOST Date (Day (`the start of week` 15 `before the expected week for` m) MINUS 175)
    AND m's `still employed in the 15th week before`
    AND m's `normal weekly earnings` AT LEAST m's `lower earnings limit`
    AND (   m's `the date in question` AT LEAST `the start of week` 11 `before the expected week for` m
         OR m's `confined before the 11th week`)

-- s 165(2) "the maternity pay period shall begin with the 11th week before
-- the expected week of confinement" (subject to prescribed later starts).
@ref s 165(2)
GIVEN m IS A `Maternity facts`
GIVETH A DATE
`the earliest start of the maternity pay period for` m MEANS
    `the start of week` 11 `before the expected week for` m

-- s 166(1)-(2): "(a) at the earnings-related rate, in respect of the first 6
-- weeks ...; and (b) at whichever is the lower of the earnings-related rate
-- and such weekly rate as may be prescribed, in respect of the remaining
-- portion"; the earnings-related rate is 90% of normal weekly earnings.
@ref s 166(1)-(2)
GIVEN m IS A `Maternity facts`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the first 6 weeks' rate for` m MEANS
    m's `normal weekly earnings` TIMES 0.9

@ref s 166(1)-(2)
GIVEN m IS A `Maternity facts`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the later weeks' rate for` m MEANS
    `the lesser of` (`the first 6 weeks' rate for` m) (m's `prescribed weekly rate`)
